<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jane Austen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jane Austen was an English novelist whose six major novels, including Pride and Prejudice (1813), Sense and Sensibility, Emma and Persuasion, portray the lives and marriages of the landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> with irony and wit. Published anonymously in her lifetime, they have become some of the most widely read works in English literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jane-austen.jpg" alt="Jane Austen" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cassandra Austen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CassandraAusten-JaneAusten(c.1810)_hires.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Writers including <b>Jane Austen</b>, Charles Dickens, the Brontë sisters, George Eliot, W.B. Yeats and James Joyce made the period a high point of literature in English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.06,-1.31&amp;year=1802" aria-label="Winchester on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4520 393 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Jane Austen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4970 663h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.06,-1.31&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife.&#8221;</p><footer>Pride and Prejudice (1813), chapter 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42671/pg42671-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is a stubbornness about me that never can bear to be frightened at the will of others. My courage always rises with every attempt to intimidate me.&#8221;</p><footer>Pride and Prejudice (1813), chapter 31 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Elizabeth Bennet &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1342/pg1342-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;All the privilege I claim for my own sex (it is not a very enviable one; you need not covet it), is that of loving longest, when existence or when hope is gone.&#8221;</p><footer>Persuasion (1817), chapter 23 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Anne Elliot &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/105/pg105-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Men have had every advantage of us in telling their own story. Education has been theirs in so much higher a degree; the pen has been in their hands.&#8221;</p><footer>Persuasion (1817), ch. 23 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Anne Elliot &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/105/pg105-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;One half of the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other.&#8221;</p><footer>Emma (1815), ch. 9 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Emma Woodhouse &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/158/pg158-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The person, be it gentleman or lady, who has not pleasure in a good novel, must be intolerably stupid.&#8221;</p><footer>Northanger Abbey (1817), ch. 14 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Henry Tilney &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/121/pg121-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
